  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The proposed link between platform architecture and structural exclusion is a theoretical model inferred from descriptive survey patterns rather than directly observed empirical causal effects.",
      "what": "A survey of 100 neighborhood food businesses in Medellín, Colombia, combined with a contextual documentary review, evaluated how small enterprises adopt digital technologies and generate community value. The study found widespread use of basic transactional tools like digital payments alongside low adoption of integrated e-commerce platforms, logistics software, and advanced management systems. Social value practices like food donations remain prevalent among these merchants but operate without formalization or digital tracking.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that digital payment adoption does not equate to platform inclusion, providing a conceptual framework that models how structural platform exclusion can systematically obscure informal, socially embedded neighborhood value creation."
    }
